WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.101
A customer wants your SaaS in their AWS account.

00:00:04.101 --> 00:00:08.658
Deployz helps small teams prepare and manage supported customer
installations.

00:00:08.658 --> 00:00:13.671
This walkthrough uses a real configuration capture and labelled product
diagrams.

00:00:13.671 --> 00:00:17.316
It does not show a new live deployment.

00:00:18.416 --> 00:00:20.821
Connect GitHub with read-only access.

00:00:20.821 --> 00:00:23.708
Deployz checks what the app needs.

00:00:23.708 --> 00:00:28.518
Review the readiness report for required values and code blockers.

00:00:28.518 --> 00:00:32.848
The report is advisory and can miss code behavior.

00:00:32.848 --> 00:00:36.216
Your team fixes relevant issues before testing.

00:00:36.216 --> 00:00:39.102
Deployz does not change the repository.

00:00:40.202 --> 00:00:43.508
This is a real resource configuration screen.

00:00:43.508 --> 00:00:48.230
It shows the web service, runtime, port, and health check.

00:00:48.230 --> 00:00:52.008
Review the detected resources and provide required settings.

00:00:52.008 --> 00:00:55.314
Build a versioned release from a commit.

00:00:55.314 --> 00:00:59.091
Test the release in your own AWS account.

00:00:59.091 --> 00:01:01.453
AWS resource charges still apply.

00:01:02.553 --> 00:01:05.485
Send your customer an installation link.

00:01:05.485 --> 00:01:10.373
They review the resources, estimated AWS costs, and requested
permissions.

00:01:10.373 --> 00:01:14.772
They approve a connector in their own AWS console.

00:01:14.772 --> 00:01:17.705
The connector calls out to Deployz.

00:01:17.705 --> 00:01:22.104
Your customer owns the resources and pays AWS directly.

00:01:22.104 --> 00:01:25.525
They do not need a Deployz account.

00:01:26.625 --> 00:01:31.241
Deployz creates the supported infrastructure and deploys the selected
release.

00:01:31.241 --> 00:01:35.395
The environment becomes ready after its health check passes.

00:01:35.395 --> 00:01:41.857
The current architecture supports one Linux x eighty six sixty four
image, an HTTP

00:01:41.857 --> 00:01:45.549
service, and optional workers from the same image.

00:01:45.549 --> 00:01:49.242
It creates a new dedicated VPC in AWS.

00:01:50.342 --> 00:01:53.270
Choose the release for each customer.

00:01:53.270 --> 00:01:56.198
Start updates and check deployment status.

00:01:56.198 --> 00:02:02.541
If a new version fails its health check, the previous version keeps
running.

00:02:02.541 --> 00:02:05.469
Rollbacks do not reverse database migrations.

00:02:05.469 --> 00:02:09.861
Review migration compatibility before you update a customer environment.

00:02:10.961 --> 00:02:12.973
Deployz receives deployment metadata.

00:02:12.973 --> 00:02:15.990
Builds run in Deployz's AWS account.

00:02:15.990 --> 00:02:20.013
The default app URL uses a Deployz proxy.

00:02:20.013 --> 00:02:24.037
App integrations can send data to external services.

00:02:24.037 --> 00:02:30.575
Subscriptions start at sixty eight US dollars per month, including one
customer environment.

00:02:30.575 --> 00:02:37.615
The fee is forty nine dollars plus nineteen dollars per billable
environment per month.

00:02:37.615 --> 00:02:39.627
AWS costs are separate.

00:02:40.727 --> 00:02:43.196
Start with a free evaluation.

00:02:43.196 --> 00:02:50.109
Connect GitHub, review readiness, and test one supported app deployment
in your own AWS

00:02:50.109 --> 00:02:50.603
account.

00:02:50.603 --> 00:02:53.072
No card or time limit.

00:02:53.072 --> 00:02:54.554
AWS charges apply.

00:02:54.554 --> 00:02:59.492
Check the architecture and pricing details at deployz dot dev.

00:02:59.492 --> 00:03:03.936
For product questions, contact help at deployz dot dev.
